WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:07.200
This page is from Alan Turing's school report&nbsp;
card. His math teacher says that "his work shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:14.480
distinct promise but he must realize that the&nbsp;
ability to put a neat and tidy solution on paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:20.640
is necessary for a first-rate mathematician"&nbsp;
and his physics teacher says that "in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:28.000
to get into Cambridge he will need to show&nbsp;
sound knowledge rather than vague ideas."

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:35.280
Now with these vague ideas Turing would go on&nbsp;
to crack the Enigma encryption that Germany&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:41.760
used during World War II and helped to end the&nbsp;
war. That was featured in the movie all about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:48.880
Turing called The Imitation Game. Turing was&nbsp;
an incredibly talented mathematician and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:54.480
one of the first computer scientists.&nbsp;
He envisioned computers as the tool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:00.800
for which we use them today and he gave one&nbsp;
of the first lectures to mention the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.200
computer intelligence, the idea from which the&nbsp;
field of artificial intelligence would grow.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:14.400
So hello I'm toby and welcome back to another&nbsp;
video where I'm going to be going through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:21.360
someone's grades. Also a thank you to The Great&nbsp;
Courses Plus for sponsoring part of this video.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:30.240
This page of Turing's report card was from 1929&nbsp;
when Turing was about 17 years old and attending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:36.720
the Sherborne School in England. His french&nbsp;
was considered fair and his proses were weak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.160
and his reading was apparently weak&nbsp;
as well. His essays showed ideas that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.520
were more grandiose than pointed. It is a&nbsp;
common thread throughout Turing's report&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.800
cards that he did much better in maths&nbsp;
and science than he did in the languages.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.720
A tutor remarks at the end of this page that they&nbsp;
are "satisfied and glad that Turing is ready to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.960
come out of his shell." That's not too bad but&nbsp;
many of the earlier report cards for Turing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:11.120
were much more harsh. This particular page&nbsp;
was the only original photocopy I could find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:17.360
but I do have a transcribed version of all of&nbsp;
the other years that Turing was at this school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.120
and some of these comments are really&nbsp;
tough. You can read through all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.600
the comments in this document yourself,&nbsp;
this is on the Sherborne School website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.280
but i'll read through some of the&nbsp;
comments that really stood out to me.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:36.720
Turing's first year at the school was in 1926&nbsp;
when he was aged 14 and it was said that he "had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:44.400
a natural bent for science but his work was badly&nbsp;
spoiled by extreme untidiness." Now this mention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:50.800
of untidy or dirty work would become a very&nbsp;
common thread through a lot of these comments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.280
His teachers used words like&nbsp;
careless and disappointing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:02.000
and they even called his lack of interest&nbsp;
in french very depressing. These pages are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.680
full of comments saying things like&nbsp;
"his work is rather lacking in life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:14.320
or that he needs to concentrate harder" or even&nbsp;
this note saying that "he is not a normal boy."

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:22.400
In mathematics he ranked 10th in his class and the&nbsp;
comment says "not very good. He spends a good deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:29.840
of time apparently in investigations of advanced&nbsp;
mathematics to the neglect of his elementary work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.840
A sound groundwork is essential in&nbsp;
any subject. His work is dirty."
 &nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.800
I guess I agree with the sentiment that you need&nbsp;
to have some good foundations before you go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:49.600
into advanced stuff but man that comment seems&nbsp;
really demoralizing for a kid who is obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:56.480
very interested in the subject. Turing was indeed&nbsp;
interested in these investigations of advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:03.600
mathematics, when he was aged 15 and a half he&nbsp;
wrote a condensed version of Einstein's general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.920
theory of relativity as a gift to his mother&nbsp;
and i'll put some pictures up of that document&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:15.760
on the screen for you to have a look at. I think&nbsp;
that it's really impressive for someone of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:21.920
age to be reading and clearly thinking about&nbsp;
some of the subjects and topics in that book.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:27.360
So I think you can clearly see he was a&nbsp;
talented kid who cared for mathematics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:34.400
but like many students I think he struggled to&nbsp;
bring this talent and enthusiasm for science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:41.440
with him through the conventional education system&nbsp;
and some of these comments from his professors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:46.000
are kind of trying to do their best to&nbsp;
really beat that passion out of him.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.200
His history teacher says "I append one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:55.200
sheet of a recent history paper as it&nbsp;
probably stays more eloquently than I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:01.280
where his weakness lies." This homework was&nbsp;
an essay that Turing wrote and I guess it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:07.440
a little messy at the start but by today's&nbsp;
standards his handwriting is pretty nice so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.760
yeah i'm not really sure maybe we'd have to have&nbsp;
a little read of that essay to truly understand.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.200
He's been at the school for a little while&nbsp;
now but is still getting told that he's too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:24.320
untidy and that there's room for improvement.&nbsp;
In science the comment is that "he has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:31.680
a lot of knowledge but his methods and practical&nbsp;
work are at present deplorable. He must learn the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:38.720
importance of scrupulous cleanliness and neatness&nbsp;
and of being able to set his work down on paper."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.640
Now it seems kind of surprising to&nbsp;
read these sorts of comments about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:49.120
Turing as an experimentalist because I mean even&nbsp;
if you've seen the movie The Imitation Game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:55.840
the machines that he dreamt up and worked on&nbsp;
were you know very neat made of many moving parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:02.560
and required a brain that could see how&nbsp;
everything fit together very neatly and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:09.840
know in a very beautiful way for the mathematics&nbsp;
to work. So I don't know if Turing improved or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.640
if you know his his talents just weren't&nbsp;
able to shine while he was at school.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:21.520
There's kind of a funny comment down here on&nbsp;
the house report saying that "no doubt he is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:28.560
very aggravating and he should know by now that I&nbsp;
don't care to find him boiling heaven knows what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:34.640
witches brew by the aid of two guttering&nbsp;
candles on a naked wooden window sill."

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:41.760
I think this is referring to Turing performing&nbsp;
one of many experiments outside of class. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:47.440
see this was a boarding school and Turing loved&nbsp;
to have his own little experiments. Not sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:53.040
what he was concocting here but yeah it sounds&nbsp;
maybe like it was even a chemistry experiment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.040 --> 00:07:00.080
By 1928 there were still plenty of struggles,&nbsp;
Turing ranked third in his class for mathematics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:04.320
although the comment says that he was&nbsp;
easily the best mathematician in the set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.200
so really he deserved to come first.

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.800
"His position is caused (again)&nbsp;
by untidiness and carelessness."

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:19.200
His english teacher says that "he should&nbsp;
read more" and that's interesting because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.560
there are also the library records of Turing&nbsp;
available on the school's website and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:31.520
can see that whilst he got out a lot of books on&nbsp;
things like math and science he only ever got out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:38.720
three fiction books and two of those were part of&nbsp;
the Alice in Wonderland series by Lewis Carroll.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:43.840
And the reason he got out those books was because&nbsp;
one of his math teachers had encouraged him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:49.840
since the author of Alice in Wonderland&nbsp;
Lewis Carroll was himself a mathematician&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:53.600
and there was a lot of math&nbsp;
sprinkled into those stories.

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:58.000
There are sentences like this one from&nbsp;
the headmaster saying that "he better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.800
take on the advice of his teachers&nbsp;
if he is to achieve any real success"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:10.720
and that kind of hurts to read because you know&nbsp;
Turing's work did go on to be so influential to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:17.760
mathematics and he definitely by any standard&nbsp;
did achieve real success in his field.

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:24.400
By 1930 though things started to look a little&nbsp;
bit more positive his physics teacher this time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:30.400
said that he is "doing much better work". The&nbsp;
headmaster says that he seems to be going well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:36.560
and the house report says that "he wins&nbsp;
respect both by brains and character."

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:40.800
The comments on pretty much every&nbsp;
subject including the languages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:46.320
are much more positive, you know we have "steady&nbsp;
and intelligent work" from the english teacher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:51.600
which is you know such a step up from those&nbsp;
very harsh comments at the start. They commend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:58.080
him for working hard and having imagination&nbsp;
and interest even in his language subjects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:05.600
This is the final page and the final comments&nbsp;
are that he is considered pleasant and friendly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:10.160
and the headmaster has appointed&nbsp;
him a school prefect. They wish him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:15.120
success at Cambridge University for&nbsp;
which he had gained a scholarship.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:19.440
I'm happy that by the end you know all&nbsp;
these teachers who had been so harsh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.240
did wish him well and commend him for&nbsp;
his intelligence and his hard work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:30.320
I don't know how much of that is Turing&nbsp;
you know becoming more mature. Maybe he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:36.800
did decide that he did you know need to be&nbsp;
a lot neater to really get his ideas across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:44.080
and after Cambridge he eventually went on to get&nbsp;
a PhD in number theory from Princeton University.

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:51.360
As a fun little bonus here are some pictures of a&nbsp;
number theory book that Turing owned and annotated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:57.840
and this book was written by John Littlewood who&nbsp;
you may remember from my previous videos about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:05.520
Ramanujan and Hardy sort of collaboration because&nbsp;
Littlewood was also involved in that story too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:09.760
So a little bit of you know crossing&nbsp;
over between these two stories.

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:14.320
Another little fun fact is that&nbsp;
Alan Turing's middle name was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:20.480
Mathison and I kind of find that fitting&nbsp;
seeing as it sounds like the son of math&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:26.960
although what would be more fitting would to&nbsp;
call Turing the father of computer science.

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:34.000
Now we can't end on that happy note though because&nbsp;
Turing's life did have a really tragic end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:41.840
In 1952 he was arrested when being&nbsp;
gay was considered a criminal offence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:47.680
He chose to be chemically castrated instead&nbsp;
of going to prison and he died two years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:54.400
later at the age of 42. He took a bite of&nbsp;
an apple which he had laced with cyanide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:59.440
perhaps a result of the depressing&nbsp;
circumstances in which he found himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:06.160
In 2009 the British government issued an apology&nbsp;
for the treatment of Alan Turing but it remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:14.240
a really sad story and a tragic end for one of&nbsp;
our greatest minds, and it remains an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:19.520
of how hatred and discrimination can&nbsp;
rob the world of wonderful people.

